13. LINKED LIST
Array:

[10][20][30][40]
Elements are stored as a continuous block conceptually.

Linked list:

[10|next] → [20|next] → [30|next] → NULL
Each node contains:

DATA + ADDRESS OF NEXT NODE
Example:

struct Node
{
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
};
Here:

data
stores the value.

next
stores the address of the next node.

14. ARRAY vs LINKED LIST
Array	Linked List
Usually contiguous memory	Nodes can be scattered
Fast random access	Sequential access
a[5] easy	Need traversal
Fixed-size concept traditionally	Dynamic growth possible
Insertion can be costly	Insertion can be easier when position/node is known
Example
Array:

10 20 30 40
Want to insert 25 between 20 and 30:

10 20 25 30 40
Elements may need shifting.

Linked list:

20 → 30
can become:

20 → 25 → 30
by changing links, assuming the required node/reference is available.

15. TIME COMPLEXITY REVISION
This is one of your important areas.

O(1)
Constant time.

printf("%d", a[5]);
One direct access.

O(n)
One loop:

for(i=0;i<n;i++)
If n doubles, work roughly doubles.

O(n²)
Nested loop:

for(i=0;i<n;i++)
{
    for(j=0;j<n;j++)
    {
    }
}
Approximately:

n × n = n²
O(log n)
Binary search.

Each step approximately halves the search space.

Example:

100 elements
↓
50
↓
25
↓
12
↓
6
↓
3
↓
1
So:

Binary Search = O(log n)

provided the data is appropriately sorted.

16. BINARY SEARCH
Suppose:

10 20 30 40 50 60 70
Find 60.

Middle:

40
60 > 40

So ignore left half.

50 60 70
Middle:

60
Found.

Key condition
Binary search requires sorted data for the standard algorithm.

Complexity
Best case: O(1)
Worst case: O(log n)
