26. LOK SABHA vs RAJYA SABHA
You previously needed more clarity here, so let's make it simple.

Lok Sabha
Think:

House of the People

Members are directly elected by voters.

Rajya Sabha
Think:

Council of States

It represents the states/UTs through its constitutional election mechanism.

Most important differences
Lok Sabha	Rajya Sabha
House of the People	Council of States
Directly elected members	Primarily indirectly elected representatives
Maximum constitutional strength 552	Maximum constitutional strength 250
Normal term 5 years	Permanent House
Can be dissolved	Cannot be dissolved
Money Bill has special role	Cannot reject a Money Bill; can only make recommendations within the constitutional time limit
⭐ Memory
LOK = PEOPLE
RAJYA = STATES
27. ARTICLE 110 — MONEY BILL
A Money Bill deals with specific matters listed in Article 110.

Very important:

Money Bill can be introduced only in Lok Sabha.

Rajya Sabha does not have the same power as Lok Sabha over a Money Bill.

The Speaker of Lok Sabha has a constitutionally important role in certifying whether a bill is a Money Bill.

Exam trap
Don't confuse:

Money Bill
with:

Every financial bill
They are not automatically the same thing.

28. ARTICLE 108 — JOINT SITTING
A joint sitting involves:

Lok Sabha + Rajya Sabha
It can be used to resolve certain deadlocks over ordinary legislation.

Important exception
A joint sitting is not used for a Money Bill.

Also, constitutional amendment bills have their own procedure and are not passed through a joint sitting mechanism.

Memory
108 = Deadlock solution for certain ordinary bills

